{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For the poor will never cease from the midst of the land</b>. The commentators said that the poor will never cease from the midst of the land at any time, for there will always be a poor person in the land — for it was revealed before Him that they would not do what He told them, as it says, \"there will be no poor among you — if only you heed the voice of YHWH your Elohim to keep and do all the commandment.\" But this is not correct in my opinion, for the Torah may allude to what will be in the future, but it does not prophesy about them explicitly that they will not fulfill the Torah and then command a commandment predicated on their transgressing the commandments forever — far be it! Rather, only by way of warning does He mention the matter. The correct interpretation is that He says \"the poor will never cease\" — meaning it is conceivable that a poor person will never be found anymore. And He mentioned this because, having promised that there would be no poor person among them when they keep all the commandment, He said: but I know that not all the generations, all the days of the world, will all of them be keeping all the commandment, such that there would be no need at all to command concerning the poor — for perhaps on some days a poor person will be found, and I command you concerning him if he is found. And He said <b>from the midst of the land</b> to allude to all of the settled world, for the promise that there will be no poor among us in the land that YHWH our Elohim gives us for an inheritance if we fulfill all the commandments there — and now He said that it is possible a poor person may be found at one of the times and in one of the places where you dwell, for the meaning of <b>in your land</b> is like the meaning of \"in all your dwellings\" (Numbers 35:29), in the land and outside the land." "And the meaning of <b>and to your poor in your land</b> is: to your poor brother and to all the poor of your land, for since the commandment pertains to Israel, He mentions \"your brother\" and needs to specify \"your brother\" and \"all the poor of your land,\" so as to command concerning the entirety of Israel. And its midrash interpretation (Sifrei 142) is to give precedence to the one who comes first. And He mentioned that there is yet a release for the slave in the seventh year of his service, for that release too is like the release mentioned above. And all of this is to recall the days of old, as I hinted in its place (Leviticus 25:2), and there it is hinted that \"and he shall be your slave forever\" (Deuteronomy 15:17) is according to its plain sense. And He returned to this commandment to explain therein the matter of the parting gift."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Ramban disputes the commentators (identified by context as Rashi and others) who read the verse as a prediction of Israel's inevitable failure to keep the Torah; he insists the verse speaks only of possibility, not certain prophecy of sin.", 1 "The reference to Sifrei 142 for the midrash 'להקדים הקודם' (to give precedence to the one who comes first) is rendered literally; the phrase concerns prioritizing a poor person who is a relative over other poor persons."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:24.590620277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}